  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Saily</strong> for a one-off eSIM with a security bundle (VPN-style ad and malware blocking) already included, backed by NordVPN's parent company. Pick <strong>Ubigi</strong> if you want a recurring monthly or annual plan for a destination you visit often, backed by an established telecom rather than a travel-focused reseller.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: One-Off Security Bundle vs. Recurring Subscription</h2>
  <p>
    Both providers price by the gigabyte, and the base rates land close together, but the products underneath are built for different situations. Saily is Nord Security's eSIM spin-off (the company behind NordVPN), and every plan bundles in ad blocking, malware and phishing site blocking, and a virtual-location tool as standard. It also sells one universal eSIM that keeps working as you cross into a new supported country, so a multi-country trip doesn't mean reinstalling at each border.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ubigi is a brand of Transatel, an MVNO enabler running cellular connectivity since the early 2000s and now part of NTT Group. Instead of a security bundle, its differentiator is plan structure: alongside one-off fixed-GB plans, it sells recurring monthly (cancellable only after a 3-month minimum) and annual (paid upfront, non-cancellable and non-refundable for the year) subscriptions. Neither Saily nor Ubigi rolls over unused data to the next period.
  </p>

