  <h2>Is Saily or Ubigi the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Saily if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You'd normally pay for a VPN or ad-blocker separately when traveling anyway</li>
        <li>Multi-country trip where you don't want a new eSIM at every border</li>
        <li>You just want a one-off plan, no recurring commitment</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with Ubigi if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You visit the same country often enough that a recurring monthly plan beats a fresh eSIM every trip</li>
        <li>You already drive a connected car from one of Ubigi's automaker partners</li>
        <li>You'd rather buy from an established MVNO than a travel-focused reseller</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Saily was blocked from new purchases in Turkey.</strong> Saily's website and app were among those blocked by Turkey's telecom regulator (BTK) starting mid-2025, alongside Holafly, Airalo, and several others. An eSIM bought and installed before landing keeps working; it just can't be purchased or topped up while physically inside the country. We haven't found the same reporting for Ubigi, so don't assume it's affected the same way without checking closer to your trip.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>A Ubigi recurring plan is a real commitment.</strong> The monthly tier locks in for three months minimum before cancellation is possible, and the annual tier can't be cancelled or refunded at all during its 12-month term. Saily carries none of that risk since every plan is a one-time purchase.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Saily vs Ubigi FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Saily or Ubigi</h3>
      <p>Country-level pricing is close, but Ubigi runs noticeably higher on regional and global plans. Ubigi's recurring subscription can work out cheaper than repeat one-off purchases for a destination visited often.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either include a VPN or security bundle</h3>
      <p>Saily does: ad blocking, malware and phishing blocking, and a virtual-location tool come standard on every plan. Ubigi has no equivalent security bundle mentioned on its site.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either offer a subscription plan</h3>
      <p>Ubigi does, with monthly and annual recurring tiers. Saily only sells one-time plans, though its Ultra tier is a separate $59.99/month subscription bundling a full NordVPN/NordPass/NordLayer/Incogni suite rather than a recurring eSIM data plan itself.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does Ubigi work in Turkey like Saily does</h3>
      <p>We've confirmed Saily was blocked from new purchases in Turkey starting mid-2025. We haven't found the same confirmation for Ubigi either way, so check closer to your trip rather than assuming.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

